/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fuel energy does not all become work.
   - Metal sets one limit: aluminium alloy pistons lose strength quickly when they run hot.
   - Temperatures inside a running engine are far from even.
   - Q is the heat carried away per second in kW; ṁ is the coolant mass flow in kg/s; c is the specific heat of the coolant in kJ/kg·K (4.18 for water); ΔT is the temperature rise of the coolant in K.
   - An air-cooled engine carries no coolant.
   - Fin spacing is a compromise.
   - The air must be made to flow.
   - Air cooling suits motorcycles, scooters, small pump sets and aircraft piston engines.
   - A liquid-cooled engine wraps the hot parts in a jacket: a hollow space cast into the block and head, running close to the liner, the valve seats and the exhaust port.
   - The circuit has four parts.
*/
