/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Hot water is lighter than cold water.
   - Δp is the driving pressure in Pa; ρc is cold-water density in kg/m³; ρh is hot-water density in kg/m³; g is 9.81 m/s²; h is the height between jacket centre and radiator centre in m.
   - The head is small, tens of pascals in a car-sized loop, so pipes are short and wide and the radiator sits well above the engine.
   - A pump, usually belt-driven from the crankshaft, pushes coolant through the jacket, radiator and heater.
   - A pressure cap is a spring-loaded valve.
   - A good coolant has a high specific heat and thermal conductivity, a low freezing point, a high boiling point, low viscosity so the pump moves it easily, no attack on iron, aluminium, copper and rubber hoses, no foaming, chemical stability o
   - Ethylene glycol (EG) and propylene glycol (PG) lower the freezing point and raise the boiling point.
*/
