/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Lubrication has more jobs than reducing friction.
   - Not all the power that the burning charge pushes onto the piston reaches the flywheel.
   - FP is friction power (kW); IP is indicated power, the power developed on the piston (kW); BP is brake power, the power measured at the crankshaft (kW).
   - Rubbing friction comes from four places.
   - Parameters that influence friction: engine speed (friction rises roughly with speed), load (ring pressure rises with cylinder pressure), oil viscosity and its temperature, bore-to-stroke ratio, number of rings, and surface finish.
   - ηm is mechanical efficiency (no unit, often written as a percentage); BP is brake power (kW); IP is indicated power (kW).
   - fmep is friction mean effective pressure (Pa or bar), a definition only; FP is friction power (W); Vd is total swept volume (m³); N is speed (rev/min); N/120 is the number of cycles each cylinder completes per second in a four-stroke engine
   - An oil film carries load in three ways, and a running engine passes through all three.
   - Hydrodynamic lubrication.
   - Boundary lubrication.
*/
